Z||000000||pHoOl||09-06-2001||11:01 AM||||Okay, so i decided to try and load RedHat.  But i figured i'd be smart and use a seperate hard drive to do it.  I have a removable bay in my system.....   anyway, i do the install, tell it to use a boot disk to have to load up.  Linux works fine... But when i went to go and put my windows Hard drive back in, it said that it was having problems loading the boot file for windows.  I tried using the NTfix, but it didn't seem to work.<P>Anyone have any ideas besides reformatting the drive.. I suppose it's an option, but not one that i'm going to consider unless i really  have to.  <P>------------------<BR>Rice mobiles are like tampons... Every p@#%y has one.<P>AIM: phool88fiero<P>        <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/phoolsig2.jpg"> ||||reg||3
Z||000001||btoth||09-06-2001||11:13 AM||||Did you plug the hard drive in the same way as it was before you installed Linux?  You really didn't need to remove the other HD.  Try removing the Linux drive and see if you can boot the drive with Windows on it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cliff Pennock||09-06-2001||11:44 AM||||Do you only have the removable drive, or do you also have another fixed disk that you normally use to boot from?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Howard_Sacks||09-06-2001||03:22 PM||||I'm not a "linux expert" but, I have a similar setup in my P3.<P>Correct me if I'm wrong here but your setup  is:<P>2 hard drives - never in at the same time<BR>one floppy drive - used for boot of linux, but not nt<P>First, what is the exact error that windows is giving you?<P>Second, make sure your bios settings for the hard disks are set to autodetect and make sure that it is picking up the proper specs on your drive. <P>Third, make sure the hard drive is mounted securely and that the ide cable is connected in the carrier for your swapable bay.  If you have two separate carriers try switching them between the linux and windows disks.  You may have a bad carrier.  I use $10 comp geeks ones and haven't had a problem yet. <A HREF="http://www.compgeeks.com/details.asp?invtid=GN210" TARGET=_blank>http://www.compgeeks.com/details.asp?invtid=GN210</A> <P><BR>In the future, there's no real reason to use a floppy boot disk if you're using a dedicated removable hard disk for linux(except for rescue).  Also, you can load NT using lilo and keep both disks in the machine at once.  I think you can even mount the NT filesystem in linux and vice versa. Info should be up somewhere on linuxdoc.org.||||reg||1
Z||000004||My7Fieros||09-06-2001||03:28 PM||||Last week on tech tv it was linux week...everything you ever wanted to know about linux...i watched it, but couldnt tell you a thing about it....I dont operate under linux. Go to <A HREF="http://www.thescreensavers.com" TARGET=_blank>www.thescreensavers.com</A> <BR>Check there for past info on linux. Hope that helps.<P>------------------<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="red"><B><I>My 7 Fieros</FONT></FONT></B></I>||||reg||1
Z||000005||pHoOl||09-06-2001||03:56 PM||||As for the exact error, i will have to check on it for you.  When you install Lilo, does it install to the hard drive or to the motherboard?  Cause i think i've eliminated anything hard drive related.  Someone told me that it's probably the master boot something that is hard wired onto the motherboard.<P>Also, can you retroactively install Lilo?  I guess that's going to be my next thing that i'm going to try.<P>Thanks for all the help guys.  Oh, and i had a paper to write last night just before all of this happened, but i was able to type it up today and we didn't even have to turn it in until tuesday, so i get to make revisions.  Guess it wasn't as much of a crisis as i thought.<P>------------------<BR>Rice mobiles are like tampons... Every p@#%y has one.<P>AIM: phool88fiero<P>        <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/phoolsig2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||LarryB||09-06-2001||05:01 PM||||LILO installs on the hard drive, as does the MBR (master boot record).  <P>Which "Windows" are you running? W98? NT4? ?? <P>When you try to boot up Windows, does LILO take control and then fails when you select to boot off your Windows partition? <P>You should also post a copy of your /etc/lilo.conf file.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by pHoOl:<BR><B>As for the exact error, i will have to check on it for you.  When you install Lilo, does it install to the hard drive or to the motherboard?  Cause i think i've eliminated anything hard drive related.  Someone told me that it's probably the master boot something that is hard wired onto the motherboard.<P>Also, can you retroactively install Lilo?  I guess that's going to be my next thing that i'm going to try.<P>Thanks for all the help guys.  Oh, and i had a paper to write last night just before all of this happened, but i was able to type it up today and we didn't even have to turn it in until tuesday, so i get to make revisions.  Guess it wasn't as much of a crisis as i thought.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||btoth||09-06-2001||05:13 PM||||You can remove Lilo from your HD by going into the "control panel" in X-windows... if you installed x-windows.  There's a check box under the Lilo panel that says to disable or something.  <P>(I haven't used Linux for a few years. )<P>Depending on which version of Windows you are running, you should be able to run the SYS command at a DOS prompt from a boot disk <B>a:\sys c:</B> and that will make your HD bootable without wiping out everything.  <BR><p>[This message has been edited by btoth (edited 09-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||pHoOl||09-06-2001||05:23 PM||||I'm running Win 2k.... so it's NT style.  Lilo never kicks in, cause i don't have lilo installed.  windows says inaccessable_boot_device  .  And that's about where i'm at.  I think maybe my next step is to try to plug my win2k hard drive into a different motherboard taht i just have lying around and see if it's the hard drive or the system.  <P>here is the Lilo.conf file<P>boot=/dev/hda<BR>map=/boot/map<BR>install=/boot/boot.b<BR>prompt<BR>timeout=50<BR>message=/boot/message<BR>linear<BR>default=linux<P>image=/boot/vmlinuz-2.4.2-SGI_XFS_1.0<BR>	label=linux<BR>	read-only<BR>	root=/dev/hdd5<BR>	append="hdb=ide-scsi"<P>other=/dev/hda1<BR>	optional<BR>	label=dos<BR><P>------------------<BR>Rice mobiles are like tampons... Every p@#%y has one.<P>AIM: phool88fiero<P>        <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/phoolsig2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Tonker||09-06-2001||06:06 PM||||I guess I'm not going to be able to be very helpful. I'm running linux, but it's a fairly standard install onto one HD with multiple partitions.<BR><BR>It is possible to dual boot win2k and linux using lilo and/or the NT loader. From your lilo.conf file, I'm guessing that you have a fixed HD as your primary master drive, and a removable drive for linux? Lilo appears to be writing to the boot sector of your fixed HD. Lilo seems to think that the first partition of your primary (fixed) HD is where it should look for the NT loader file. It could be that if you're also using a removable drive for win2k and they're not in at the same time, that your lilo.conf for dos should read something like:<BR><BR>other=/dev/hdd1<BR>optional<BR>label=dos<BR><BR>The reason I say this is because lilo probably doesn't understand that it should be booting from the same 'device' but a different 'media'. This is purely speculation on my part and may, in fact, have no basis in reality. I'd seek some input from wiser minds first.<BR><BR>If you can boot into linux, the first thing I'd do is make a floppy boot disk if you don't already have one. That way if you have to do a boot sector repair on your primary HD you'll still be able to get into linux. <BR><BR>You might try <A HREF="http://www.helpfromtechs.com" TARGET=_blank>www.helpfromtechs.com</A>  and ask in the 'other operating systems' forum.<BR><BR>Good luck!<p>[This message has been edited by Tonker (edited 09-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||LarryB||09-06-2001||06:26 PM||||2 dumb questions: are there more than 1 partitions on that W2K drive, and if so, is the desired boot partition the active partition on the drive?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||btoth||09-06-2001||09:04 PM||||OK, I'm running Win2k.  Did you try booting off of the Win2k cd and trying using the stupid Repair feature  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">?? If your Win2k HD is formated using FAT32 instead of NTFS (at least I think it only works with FAT) try making a Win98 boot disk and include Fdisk, Sys, Format, Scandisk, and Edit. (Makes a good error-fixing bootdisk).  The Win98 boot disk will bypass the Win2k security unlike the Win2k boot disk so that just incase you can't get it to work, you can at least access your user folders and back things up.  But try running the Sys command, and check Fdisk to see if your Windows drive is set up as bootable.  (Try doing this with the linux drive out of the computer.)||||reg||1
Z||000012||thomas_l||09-07-2001||10:14 AM||||Your Linux install changed the boot disk (the fixed disk). Read this <A HREF="http://www.linuxdoc.org/HOWTO/MultiOS-HOWTO.html" TARGET=_blank>HOWTO</A> for directions. Or buy Partition Magic and install BootMagic (easier, but not free).||||reg||1
Z||000013||GodSend||09-07-2001||12:20 PM||||If it comes down to it... Boot into Linix (goto the console) and uninstall lilo.... Sorry I cant remember the command.. may be <P>lilo /u<P>type <P>man lilo<P>and see what it tells you..<P> I had the exact same problem... be warned.. you will not be able to get back into linux after this (you will need a boot disk, or reinstall)..  good luck..||||reg||1
Z||000014||Howard_Sacks||09-07-2001||12:37 PM||||Phool,<P>Was your NT drive in the machine when you installed linux?  I had assumed you pulled it before the install becuase you said "put my windows Hard drive back in"  But your lilo.conf file shows two different drives?  If your nt disk is in the machine at the same time as the linux disk, while in linux run cfdisk (just open a terminal as root and type cfdisk) write down on paper the names of your partitions and their types and sizes.  I'll try to help you from there.<P>The master boot record is not hard wired to the motherboard (where do people come up with this stuff :-) ). And like Larry said, it installs LILO to the hard disk.  Usually the master boot record unless you tell the installer you want it somewhere else.  Do not remove lilo.  If you remove it you will not be able to boot into linux without a hastle.<P>If you need a boot floppy to boot into linux, that is most likely where lilo is installed.  You're removing the linux boot floppy before trying to load windows, right?<P>The install does not do anything to your motherboard, but like I said before, if your moherboard's bios isn't set to auto detect, when you pull the linux drive out, your machine might do some funny things.  Make sure in BIOS that the cyls, heads and sectors are correct for the drive that is in the machine. And that it is not trying to pick up the linux disk when you're trying to boot windows.<P>OK, you're running SGI's XFS distro, which is based on Redhat but not exactly Redhat. I'm not sure if they updated this on the final version, but in the betas I tried of it they do non-standard things with the partition maps.  Also, unrelated to your problem, I know having a journaling filesystem is cool, but I'm not sure that it's worth the bloat that the SGI kernel adds. <P>tonker: hdd1 might be right for your setup, but not every linux install is the same. Also, after you change lilo.conf you need to run lilo or when you reboot, it is not pretty. You need to tell newbies minor details like that.<P>thomas: I don't think that he changed the boot disk unless he manually changed it in bios.  Buying partition magic before he does something like this again wouldn't be a bad idea though.<P>Maybe next time you should buy a whole separate machine for linux :-) Deepspacetech usually has good stuff cheap. <A HREF="http://www.deepspacetech.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.deepspacetech.com/</A>   All you really need to get started having fun with linux is first generation pentium. Or even a 386 if you're not going to be using X.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||pHoOl||09-07-2001||01:46 PM||||Okay, a little bit of an update.  Last night me and a buddy spent three hours working on this darn thing.  Got windows 98 (may as well kill myself) working, and also got linux working on a seperate hard drive.  Win98 can't connect to the internet, cause the stupid connection wizard can't be loaded.  Talk about why i hate 98, i have it loaded for five minutes and already it doesn't work.<P>So i tried to do the 2000 install, but it was freezing in the middle of the install.  This was all at about 2am last night/this morning.  I decided to drop the Linux hard drive in since i can at least get to the internet and email on it.  So there it stands.<P>Oh, this is the part that i think i forgot to tell yall, i had a 10 gig drive that was staying in there all the time (secondary) that had all my mp3z on it and stuff.  but yeah, when i tried mounting it with linux yesterday it evidently seemed to make it so that i couldnt' access it.<P>To make a short story boring, i lost about 2000 songs.  Can we have a moment of silence?  So, if anyone would like to send me mp3s or their entire collection, let me know.  Help a brotha out.<P>------------------<BR>Rice mobiles are like tampons... Every p@#%y has one.<P>AIM: phool88fiero<P>        <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/phoolsig2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
